## Path 1 — steering Claude

The normal path. Open Claude Code in the repo and drive it with the workflow commands:

```
/draft feature "let users export their data"   → captures the idea in the backlog
/plan FEAT-012                                 → turns it into a spec, asks everything up front
/implement FEAT-012                            → tests + code per subtask, gated each time
/verify FEAT-012                               → full gate, review, manual smoke test
/release minor                                 → version, changelog, publish, deploy
```

`/ship FEAT-012` runs that whole chain in one go. `/resume` picks up interrupted work.

Two things worth knowing:

- **Questions come up front.** `/plan` batches every open decision into one message before implementation starts, so you can answer once and walk away.
- **The gate is not optional and not manual.** Claude runs `scripts/ci.sh` itself before every commit and before any merge or release. You do not need to ask for it.

## Path 2 — writing the code yourself

Also fine. The rules are the same ones the workflow enforces:

**Before you push:** run `scripts/ci.sh fast`. Before opening a PR or merging: `scripts/ci.sh full`. This is the same script CI runs — if it passes locally it passes there.

**Commit messages** follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```
feat(auth): add OAuth login with Google
fix(session): correct token expiry calculation
```

Types: `feat` · `fix` · `refactor` · `test` · `docs` · `chore` · `perf` · `ci`. Imperative mood, lowercase, no trailing period, ≤72 characters on the first line.

**Branches:** `feature/feat-001-short-title`, `fix/bug-042-short-description`, `chore/update-dependencies`.

**Code conventions** live where they are needed: `src/CLAUDE.md` for source, `tests/CLAUDE.md` for tests. They apply to human contributions too.

## How CI works here

`scripts/ci.sh` is the single definition of "does this pass" — the GitHub workflows call it, Claude calls it, you call it. There is no separate list of checks that can drift out of sync.

| | `fast` | `full` |
|---|---|---|
| runs | format, lint, types, affected tests | everything, plus integration/e2e and the build |
| when | every commit | before a merge, a PR, a release |

**Why you will see `[skip ci]` on Claude's commits.** Claude runs the identical gate locally before committing, so re-running it in Actions would spend minutes proving something already proven. The marker suppresses that duplicate run — and it comes with an obligation: Claude must actually run `ci.sh full` before any merge or release, never skip both.

**Your commits carry no such marker, so CI runs on them normally.** The skip applies only to commits the workflow itself produced. If you push by hand, expect the full pipeline — that is the intended safety net for human contributions.

The workflows for PR, release and deploy are maintained alongside the code: when the gate or the release steps change, the workflow files change in the same ticket.

## Where things are

| | |
|---|---|
| `docs/VISION.md` | What this project is for, and what it deliberately is not |
| `docs/specs/` | Tickets — `backlog/`, `ready/`, `completed/` |
| `docs/dev/architecture.md` | How it is built |
| `docs/dev/setup.md` | Running it locally or against the dev deployment |
| `docs/user/` | User-facing documentation |
| `CHANGELOG.md` | The **current major**'s released changes. Older majors are archived under `docs/changelog/`, split at version headings so no release is ever cut in half — the root file stays where tooling expects it, the archives do not belong there |

